    "https://uva.rekrytointi.com/paikat/index.php?jid=1131&key=&o=A_RJ&rspvt=fxfuun07520c0g4gw0go0880kokok44": {
      "title": "Senior Specialist in Research Funding",
      "description": "Senior Specialist in Research Funding ▼ We use cookies to improve the user experience of the service and for statistics. By selecting \"Accept all\" you give your consent to the use of cookies. Modify settings Necessary (required cookies for the service to work) Statistics (cookies that measure the use of the service) suomeksi på svenska Back to the job list Want to update your application? Log in. Senior Specialist in Research Funding The University of Vaasa is an inspiring and international community where we work together for a sustainable and responsible future. We combine expertise in business and technology in a unique way, tackling current challenges with determination and collaboration. As part of the Nordic region’s largest energy technology hub, we promote sustainable business, energy, and society. We educate future professionals who will create a fairer and more sustainable world. The University of Vaasa is seeking a Senior Specialist in Research Funding for a permanent position within the Research Services and Graduate School , which is responsible for providing and developing services and processes that support research activities and doctoral education. Our areas of expertise include research funding, responsible and open science, science and research policy, and doctoral training. The preferred starting date is October-November 2026 , or as agreed. Your Role The University of Vaasa is strengthening its focus on international research funding, with a particular objective of increasing success in securing European Research Council (ERC) and Horizon Europe funding in the coming years. In this role, you will contribute to the preparation and development of research funding applications in close collaboration with individual researchers and research groups. You will support researchers throughout the entire proposal development process, from the initial idea stage to proposal submission, including budget preparation. Project preparation activities focus on funding under the EU Framework Programme for Research and Innovation, particularly support for Horizon Europe Pillar I ERC calls and Pillar II thematic calls. You will also contribute to the university’s EU advocacy activities and the strategic development of research funding in close cooperation with researchers, university leadership, other support services, and external stakeholders. Additional responsibilities include liaising with research funding organizations, identifying suitable funding opportunities and communicating them to researchers, guiding, advising, and coaching researchers applying for EU funding and participating in other unit activities depending on organizational needs, your expertise, and professional interests. What We Expect from You Required Qualifications and Experience: A relevant master's degree or equivalent postgraduate degree Knowledge of EU funding programmes (especially ERC and Horizon Europe Pillar II) Experience in preparing and managing applications for EU-funded projects Excellent English language skills an d fluent written and spoken Finnish Ability to work effectively under pressure and prioritize tasks, as the work can at times be deadline-driven and intensive Strong writing, communication, interpersonal, and networking skills Ability to manage multiple simultaneous projects and work under tight deadlines Considered an Advantage: Doctoral degree (PhD) Knowledge of EU networks and stakeholder communities Initiative, flexibility, service-mindedness, and a goal-oriented, solution-focused approach to work We offer a diverse and challenging position supported by a team of experienced research funding specialists. This multifaceted role provides excellent opportunities for professional growth and to further develop your expertise. At the University of Vaasa, we prioritize your well-being and professional growth. We offer comprehensive occupational health services, sports and cultural benefits, and plenty of opportunities for continuous learning. Work-life balance matters to us – we offer flexible working hours and the possibility to work partly remotely. Salary The salary will be based on the Finnish University Salary System for expert and support staff. The monthly salary is based on job demand levels 10–11 (3883-4429 €/kk) . An additional personal salary component is paid based on work performance. How to Apply To apply for the position, please use our online application system (using the \"Submit an application\" button below). The application must include a CV and a free form cover letter. The application, including annexes, has to be in our online application database by midnight September 6, 2026 (EEST). Further Information For more information about the position, please contact: Mathias Björklund, Head of Research Services, tel. +358 29 449 8760, email: firstname.lastname(a)uwasa.fi Inquiries about the online application system: careers(a)uwasa.fi The employment is subject to a probationary period. ********* The University of Vaasa’s 7,000 students and 650 employees enjoy Finland’s most beautiful campus by the sea. Together, we form a thriving and diverse university community where everyone feels they belong. Working with us means meaningful tasks, flexible working arrangements, comprehensive occupational health services, and employee benefits that support well-being and recovery. Our campus offers modern workspaces and inspiring meeting areas for smooth collaboration. The international HR Excellence in Research award reflects our commitment to high-quality HR practices. Learn more about us: https://www.uwasa.fi/en/open-positions Application period starts: 2026-08-28 10:30 Application period ends: 2026-09-06 23:59 Submit an application Share Direct link to the job: https://uva.rekrytointi.com/paikat/index.php?jid=1131&o=A_RJ Privacy policy Accessibility Cookies"
    },
    "https://uva.rekrytointi.com/paikat/index.php?jid=1125&key=&o=A_RJ&rspvt=fxfuun07520c0g4gw0go0880kokok44": {
      "title": "Project Researcher (PhD. Student) or Postdoctoral Researcher, engine emission aftertreatment modelling, simulation, experiments and control (Energy Technology)",
      "description": "Project Researcher (PhD. Student) or Postdoctoral Researcher, engine emission aftertreatment modelling, simulation, experiments and control (Energy Technology) ▼ We use cookies to improve the user experience of the service and for statistics. By selecting \"Accept all\" you give your consent to the use of cookies. Modify settings Necessary (required cookies for the service to work) Statistics (cookies that measure the use of the service) suomeksi på svenska Back to the job list Want to update your application? Log in. Project Researcher (PhD. Student) or Postdoctoral Researcher, engine emission aftertreatment modelling, simulation, experiments and control (Energy Technology) The University of Vaasa is an inspiring and international community where we work together for a sustainable and responsible future. We combine expertise in business and technology in a unique way, tackling current challenges with determination and collaboration. As part of the Nordic region’s largest energy technology hub, we promote sustainable business, energy, and society. We educate future professionals who will create a fairer and more sustainable world. The position is offered initially for two (2) years starting on January 2027 or at an earliest possible date mutually agreed between both parties. The position is located in the School of Technology and Innovation and under energy technology discipline. Your role You will be part of the Efficient Powertrain Solutions (EPS) Research group working on several challenging projects, at the forefront of research and development of novel combustion engine technologies.  With over 40 researchers and 3 MEUR of annual research turnover, we are the biggest focused powertrain-oriented research group in Finland. Our focus is on model-based development of advanced engine solutions supporting our industry partners in conforming to tightening emissions and noise regulation in marine and off-road sectors. Hydrogen represents a promising zero-carbon alternative fuel for large oceangoing vessels, where internal combustion engines are expected to remain a prime mover. Hydrogen-biogas combustion is a green solution in marine application, where the methane slip in exhaust gases is challenging to remove by catalytic and thermal management methods, which require further development. Methane is counted parallel to CO2 as a greenhouse gas, which emissions are regulated. In addition, significant challenges have restrained its large-scale onboard integration, catalyst durability, amid concerns related to safety, fuel availability, and cost. This research is related to green shift in off-road applications (marine, machinery), where conventional fossil fuels like diesel oil will be replaced with low-/zero-carbon fuels with flexible mixtures. The new EU-funded H4PERION project directly addresses these barriers by developing a new ultra-efficient hydrogen-capable internal combustion engine (TRL 7, while meeting 55% thermal efficiency target), followed by concept integration at ship level through a pilot demonstration. In this context as a part of the EPS team, you will contribute to: Developing novel catalytic methane removal solutions securing low pollutant emissions and flexible operation with hydrogen and other gaseous fuels. 1D modelling and simulations of catalytic aftertreatment systems based on engine-out emissions and experimental catalyst performance results. Experimental catalyst testing campaign in corresponding off-road application. Integrating the exhaust aftertreatment systems together with appropriate thermal and energy management strategies to ensure compliance with the project’s emission and efficiency targets. Achieving the targeted efficiency within the project timeline requires deep integration of catalyst simulation and testing environments with autonomous calibration routines. To this end we are seeking talented and motivated researchers in simulation-driven catalytic aftertreatment development and optimization. All tasks are agreed in more detail in the Annual Work Plan. The annual working time is 1612 hours. Requirements You have a master’s degree or a PhD in a relevant field. For example chemical/mechanical/process engineering, with a specialization in emission catalyst reaction reactions/modelling or combustion engines. You have documented experience in 1D aftertreatment/combustion model calibration and validation with experimental data. Experience in 3D simulation environments is a benefit. You are able to efficiently plan and conduct engine laboratory experiments and postprocess emission data. Laboratory experience such as test-rig development, instrumentation, and data-acquisition is considered a strong advantage. Engine emission aftertreatment involves chemical reactions, as a result good knowledge on catalysts as well as chemical reaction mechanisms and kinetics is a benefit. Excellent analytical skills in addition to good academic writing skills are highly advantageous for your candidature. Proven track record in high-quality journals/conferences/projects on the topics related to the position, is an additional advantage. You enjoy developing innovative solutions to technically challenging problems and have the right mix of intellectual curiosity and practical pragmatism. You have good communication and language (English is required) skills and enjoy working both independently and as part of a team. You are open to sharing your knowledge with colleagues and ambitious to develop yourself as a researcher. To be eligible for a full PhD student position, the candidate is required to participate in a separate application process which is arranged at least twice a year in order to be admitted and enrolled as a doctoral student at the University of Vaasa. For more information about eligibility and the application process, please visit the Graduate School's website: Doctoral education | University of Vaasa (uwasa.fi) Inspirational work environment In the University of Vaasa, we conduct combustion engine research focused on advanced energy concepts and alternative fuels. We have a special focus on large bore-marine and stationary engines, for which we have a unique, state of the art, research facilities at EnergyLab . With close collaboration with industry, we are transforming the energy sector by bringing the cutting-edge technology to real-world application. We welcome you to join one of the most fascinating energy innovation centers in the Nordic region in combustion modelling and control. For more information about the facilities see: EnergyLab . Efficient Powertrain Solutions (EPS) research group is part of Energy Technology track in the School of Technology and Innovations. We need open-minded, creative and skilled individuals to explore and develop the future energy solutions. We continuously develop all of our operations in cooperation with companies and other partners. We are strongly networked and responsible actors in research, education and public relations. As a work community, our team is diverse, international and encouraging. The cooperation and support of colleagues promotes well-being and inspires working. More information about the School . We offer We have a great value to both your personal and your professional development. Many opportunities exist for you to develop: conferences, courses, workshops, coaching, mentoring and international internships. The salary will be based on both the job requirement and the employee’s personal performance in accordance with the Finnish University Salary System. The monthly salary of a Project Researcher (PhD Student) is based on job demand levels 2-4 (€ 2331-2907) and Postdoctoral Researcher on level 5 (€ 3366). An additional personal salary component is included based on work performance. How to apply To apply for this position, please use our online application system (using the “Submit an application” button below). The application, including appendices, has to be submitted using our online application database no later than 30th of September 2026. The application should include the following annexes in English (See instructions concerning these annexes: https://www.uwasa.fi/en/open-positions/application-attachments ): Curriculum vitae Incl. list of Publications (for postdoctoral researcher position) Additional attachment 1. Single-page cover letter describing your motivation for this topic of research Additional attachment 2. Transcript of study records in a PDF format Further information about the position: Primary contact: Teuvo Maunula - Professor of practice; Catalytic Aftertreatment Systems, Energy Technology, School of Technology and Innovations Tel. +358 29 449 8431 Prof. Maciej Mikulski - Professor; Energy Technology, Research Group Leader Efficient Powertrain Solutions. Tel. +358 29 449 8591 Email: firstname.lastname (a) uwasa.fi The employment is subject to a 6 month probationary period. Inquiries about the online application system: careers (a) uwasa.fi ____________________________________________________________________________________ The University of Vaasa’s 7,000 students and 700 employees enjoy Finland’s most beautiful campus by the sea. Together, we form a thriving and diverse university community where everyone feels they belong. Working with us means meaningful tasks, flexible working arrangements, comprehensive occupational health services, and employee benefits that support well-being and recovery. Our campus offers modern workspaces and inspiring meeting areas for smooth collaboration. The international HR Excellence in Research award reflects our commitment to high-quality HR practices. Learn more about us: https://www.uwasa.fi/en/open-positions Application period starts: 2026-08-31 13:00 Application period ends: 2026-09-30 23:59 Submit an application Share Direct link to the job: https://uva.rekrytointi.com/paikat/index.php?jid=1125&o=A_RJ Privacy policy Accessibility Cookies"
    },
    "https://uva.rekrytointi.com/paikat/index.php?jid=1133&key=&o=A_RJ&rspvt=fxfuun07520c0g4gw0go0880kokok44": {
      "title": "Project Researcher (PhD. Student) or Postdoctoral Researcher, Electricity Markets and Flexibility (Electrical Engineering)",
      "description": "Project Researcher (PhD. Student) or Postdoctoral Researcher, Electricity Markets and Flexibility (Electrical Engineering) - 2 positions ▼ We use cookies to improve the user experience of the service and for statistics. By selecting \"Accept all\" you give your consent to the use of cookies. Modify settings Necessary (required cookies for the service to work) Statistics (cookies that measure the use of the service) suomeksi på svenska Back to the job list Want to update your application? Log in. Project Researcher (PhD. Student) or Postdoctoral Researcher, Electricity Markets and Flexibility (Electrical Engineering) - 2 positions The University of Vaasa is an inspiring and international community where we work together for a sustainable and responsible future. We combine expertise in business and technology in a unique way, tackling current challenges with determination and collaboration. As part of the Nordic region’s largest energy technology hub, we promote sustainable business, energy, and society. We educate future professionals who will create a fairer and more sustainable world. The University of Vaasa is seeking two motivated and talented Project Researchers or Postdoctoral Researcher to join the Research Council of Finland-funded Electricity Markets and Flexibility project. The positions are full-time and fixed-term for three (3) years. The preferred starting date is flexible but agreed together as soon as possible. The positions are located in the School of Technology and Innovations and under electrical engineering discipline. The positions are located in Vaasa. Background The Electricity Markets and Flexibility project investigates how electricity markets must be designed, and how demand-side flexibility must be mobilised, for a power system dominated by variable renewable generation to remain reliable and affordable. The project combines quantitative modelling of markets and the wider energy system with empirical analysis of how flexibility is created, priced, and traded by market participants. A central objective is to understand the interaction between market design choices and the total cost of the energy system, and to quantify the value of flexible demand, including the rapidly growing load from data centres, to system operation, investment, and consumers. The project will produce new knowledge on electricity market design, system-level cost assessment, and demand-side flexibility, as well as new datasets, modelling methods, and blueprints. The results can be directly utilised in the scientific community, but also to improve decision-making in the public and private sectors. The two positions are divided between two closely connected research themes. Decided theme is chosen depending on the profiles of the selected candidates. Theme A: Electricity market design and the cost of the whole energy system Research on this theme addresses the design of wholesale, balancing, reserve, and capacity markets under high shares of variable renewable generation; price formation, scarcity pricing, and locational signals; network charging and cost allocation; and sector coupling with heat and transport. The work involves system-level modelling of investment and operation to assess how alternative market designs affect the total cost of the energy system, security of supply, and the distribution of costs and benefits among market participants. Theme B: Power system flexibility in data centres Research on this theme addresses large data centres as controllable electricity loads. Topics include temporal and spatial shifting of computing workloads, the use of on-site energy storage and backup capacity, thermal flexibility and waste heat recovery, participation in wholesale, balancing, and reserve markets, grid connection and connection agreements, and the techno-economic and business case assessment of flexible operation. The work spans the modelling of data centre flexibility potential and its valuation from the perspectives of the operator, the system operator, and society. Your role You will play a key role in the project team and actively participate in the implementation of different activities within the research project. The role emphasises the ability to conduct high-quality research and interact with different national and international stakeholders. Your work will contribute directly to project deliverables and high-quality academic publications. Key responsibilities in the position: Contribute to the project’s activities. Conduct independent and collaborative research within one of the project’s research themes. Develop, apply, and document quantitative models and analyses of electricity markets, energy systems, or flexible demand. Collaborate with an interdisciplinary group of universities, research organisations, system operators, energy companies, technology providers, and data centre operators. Participate in data collection, analysis, and validation activities with industry stakeholders and policy makers. Lead and co-author high-quality scientific publications in international peer-reviewed journals. Support teaching or supervision tasks when applicable (according to university guidelines). Present research at academic conferences and project meetings. Produce a doctoral dissertation based on the research carried out within the project. The position may also include other tasks to be agreed upon separately. All tasks are agreed in more detail in the Annual Work Plan. The annual working time is 1612 hours. Requirements We expect the selected person to have the ability and motivation for doctoral studies and to complete a doctoral degree. To be eligible for a full PhD student position, the candidate is required to participate in a separate application process which is arranged at least twice a year in order to be admitted and enrolled as a doctoral student at the University of Vaasa. For more information about eligibility and the application process, please visit the Graduate School's website: Doctoral education | University of Vaasa (uwasa.fi) Required: You have a master’s degree or a PhD in a relevant field. Strong proven interest in the research area of the project. Demonstrated quantitative and analytical skills, including programming or modelling experience. Ability to work both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team. Motivation to produce high-quality research outputs. Excellent communication skills in English. Desirable: Experience with energy system, power system, or electricity market modelling, and with the associated software and programming environments. Experience with optimisation, simulation, econometrics, or data analysis applied to energy or market data. Knowledge of data centre operations, computing infrastructure, or ICT energy use (Theme B). Experience working with industry stakeholders. Prior research or publication experience. We offer We offer an exciting opportunity to join a high-profile Research Council of Finland funded project as a Project Researcher or Postdoctoral Researcher for three years. The position provides excellent opportunities for international collaboration with leading experts in the field, access to industry-driven case studies, real-world data, and state-of-the-art modelling tools. For the Project Researcher position, the successful candidate will be expected to apply for and pursue a doctoral degree in Electrical Engineering through the University of Vaasa’s Doctoral Programme following a separate application process. You will benefit from comprehensive training in research methods, doctoral studies, and professional development, while working in a supportive and inspiring research environment within the University of Vaasa’s vibrant research community. We promote the overall well-being of our employees at work by, among other things, offering fitness and cultural benefits, extensive occupational health care services, and versatile opportunities for continuous learning. Flexible working time arrangements and the possibility of partial remote work also support the balance of work and free time. The salary will be based on both the job requirement and the employee’s personal performance in accordance with the Finnish University Salary System. The monthly salary of a Project Researcher (PhD Student) is based on job demand levels 2-4 (€ 2331-2907) and Postdoctoral Researcher on level 5 (€ 3366). An additional personal salary component is included based on work performance. How to apply To apply for this position, please use our online application system (using the “Submit an application” button below). The application, including appendices, has to be submitted using our online application database no later than 30th of September 2026. The application should include the following annexes in English (See instructions concerning these annexes: https://www.uwasa.fi/en/open-positions/application-attachments ): Curriculum vitae Incl. list of Publications (for postdoctoral researcher position) Use of CV template is required Additional attachment 1. Single-page cover letter describing your motivation and fit for the position, and indicating which research theme you are applying for Additional attachment 2. Transcript of study records and certificates in a PDF format Additional Attachment 3. A writing sample (e.g., thesis, essay, or publication) To ensure a smooth and fair recruitment process, we encourage applicants to focus on sharing information that is relevant to the selection process in their application. If you choose to include personal or sensitive details in your application, please do so only if you are comfortable sharing that information with, for example, external experts. Further information about the position: Assoc. Prof. Sinan Kufeoglu; Electrical Engineering Email: sinan.kufeoglu (a) uwasa.fi The employment is subject to a 6 month probationary period. Inquiries about the online application system: careers (a) uwasa.fi ____________________________________________________________________________________ The University of Vaasa’s 7,000 students and 700 employees enjoy Finland’s most beautiful campus by the sea. Together, we form a thriving and diverse university community where everyone feels they belong. Working with us means meaningful tasks, flexible working arrangements, comprehensive occupational health services, and employee benefits that support well-being and recovery. Our campus offers modern workspaces and inspiring meeting areas for smooth collaboration. The international HR Excellence in Research award reflects our commitment to high-quality HR practices. Learn more about us: https://www.uwasa.fi/en/open-positions Application period starts: 2026-09-03 12:00 Application period ends: 2026-09-30 23:59 Submit an application Share Direct link to the job: https://uva.rekrytointi.com/paikat/index.php?jid=1133&o=A_RJ Privacy policy Accessibility Cookies"
    }
